-- title:
--   Finiteness of the cutoff weight against the fractional kernel
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$, let $\delta > 0$, and let $\alpha$ satisfy $0 < \alpha < 2$. Then
--
--   $$\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \min\!\Big(\frac{\|u\|^2}{\delta^2},\,1\Big)\,\|u\|^{-d-\alpha}\,du \;<\; \infty .$$
--
--   Here $\min(\|u\|^2/\delta^2, 1)$ is the factor contributed by a Lipschitz cutoff of width $\delta$, and $\|u\|^{-d-\alpha}$ is the fractional jump kernel of order $\alpha$ written in the difference variable $u = x-y$.
--
--   Both restrictions on $\alpha$ are used, and at opposite ends of the range: near the origin the cutoff supplies the factor $\|u\|^2/\delta^2$, which tames the singularity exactly when $2 - d - \alpha > -d$, that is when $\alpha < 2$; near infinity the cutoff factor is the constant $1$ and the integrand is $\|u\|^{-d-\alpha}$, integrable exactly when $\alpha > 0$. This finiteness is the constant appearing on the right of the cutoff error estimates, and it is what makes the cutoff argument quantitative.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The integral is a lower Lebesgue integral of an `ENNReal.ofReal` integrand over all of $\mathbb{R}^d$; the value at $u = 0$, where $\|u\|^{-d-\alpha}$ is a junk value in Lean, is irrelevant since $\{0\}$ is null.
